{"kind":"request","text":"codex: hugh hit a resident-lifecycle bug and it has two halves. Repro: run gs serve, stop it with Ctrl+C (the process exits cleanly), then run gs serve again — it refuses with \"refusing to serve: the service holding this repository could not be shown to be gone, so its claim is left alone (http://127.0.0.1:PORT); if you are certain no service is running, remove the claim with git update-ref -d refs/gitseq/resident/GENESIS\". The old service was genuinely gone, but the claim ref persisted and the startup check could not prove absence, so it refused and pointed the operator at a manual ref deletion. The manual-delete hint is actively dangerous: I probed a claimed port during triage and a service WAS answering (an operator's restart), so blindly following the hint would delete a live resident's claim and let a second resident race the same log. hugh named the two fixes and both are right.","body":{"conditions":"Fix the resident claim lifecycle in gs serve (cmd/gs + the resident in internal/service). TWO parts. (1) SHUTDOWN CLEANUP: gs serve must install a SIGINT/SIGTERM handler that removes its own claim ref (refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e) and any lock before exit, so a clean Ctrl+C leaves no stale claim. This is necessary but not sufficient, because SIGKILL and crashes cannot be caught. (2) ACTIVE LIVENESS PROBE ON STARTUP (the backstop, and the more important safety fix): when gs serve finds an existing claim, it must PROBE the claimed address (an HTTP GET to the resident status endpoint, e.g. /v0/status, with a short timeout) rather than only inspecting the ref. On a clean connection-refused at a loopback address, the prior service is provably gone: reclaim automatically and log that it did so (do not require a manual git update-ref -d). On a reachable response, refuse and say so precisely — the message must state that a service IS answering at \u003caddr\u003e (and, when the address is loopback, name the listening pid if resolvable) rather than the ambiguous 'could not be shown to be gone', and it must NOT suggest deleting the claim while a service answers. Only on an ambiguous result (timeout, non-loopback address that cannot be safely probed) fall back to the conservative refusal, and even then frame the manual override as last-resort with an explicit warning that deleting a live service's claim races the log. Include: tests for clean-shutdown-removes-claim; startup-after-clean-shutdown reclaims without manual intervention; startup-with-live-service refuses and does not offer deletion; startup-after-SIGKILL (simulate by leaving the claim with no listener) auto-reclaims via the refused probe; and the ambiguous/non-loopback path stays conservative. Also fix the observed leak of orphaned gs serve processes from test/publish-chain harnesses if it shares the same root cause (serve not terminating on signal). Layer: application/CLI + resident; no kernel/fold change. Exact clean pushed head, full gates, per-path artifacts, independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review, and update docs/reference/gs/serve.md for the new reclaim behaviour. Do not merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}